      <h1>Wolverhampton Taxi Badge Test: Practice Questions &amp; Answers</h1>

      <p>
        Want to know what the exam really asks? Here are seven
        <strong>Wolverhampton taxi test practice questions</strong> — one from
        each of the seven categories on the real PHV test. Try each one
        yourself first, then click "Show answer" to check and read a
        plain-English explanation. These are genuine questions from the free
        tier of the PHV Prep UK app, not made-up examples.
      </p>

      <p>
        Before you start, it helps to know how the test works: 28 questions,
        45 minutes, and you must score at least 21 out of 28
        <strong>and</strong> get all 8 key questions right. Our guide on
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/how-to-pass/"
          >how to pass the Wolverhampton PHV test</a
        >
        covers the full picture.
      </p>

      <h2>Safeguarding</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>If you believe a child you are carrying may be a victim of
        sexual exploitation, who should you contact?</strong>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Wait until you finish your shift</li>
        <li>The police</li>
        <li>Just the parents</li>
        <li>Nobody, it's not your concern</li>
      </ul>
      <details>
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <p>
          <strong>Correct answer: The police.</strong> All licensed drivers
          have a duty to safeguard children. If you suspect child sexual
          exploitation, contact the police (999 if urgent, 101 otherwise)
          without delay.
        </p>
      </details>

      <h2>Plying for Hire</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>Can a private hire driver pick up a member of the public who
        flags them down in the street?</strong>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Yes if the customer agrees to pay</li>
        <li>No</li>
        <li>Yes if the meter is off</li>
        <li>Only outside city centre</li>
      </ul>
      <details>
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <p>
          <strong>Correct answer: No.</strong> A PHV must only carry
          passengers whose journey has been pre-booked through a licensed
          operator. Picking up flag-downs is illegal plying for hire.
        </p>
      </details>

      <h2>Disability Awareness</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>When can you charge extra for carrying an assistance dog with
        a disabled passenger?</strong>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Never — it must be carried free of charge</li>
        <li>Always — animals are extra</li>
        <li>Only if the dog is large</li>
        <li>Only if you have to clean afterwards</li>
      </ul>
      <details>
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <p>
          <strong>Correct answer: Never — it must be carried free of
          charge.</strong> Under the Equality Act 2010, drivers must carry
          assistance dogs free of charge. Charging extra is a criminal offence
          with an unlimited fine.
        </p>
      </details>

      <p>
        Those first three categories — safeguarding, plying for hire and
        disability awareness — are where
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/8-key-questions/"
          >the 8 key questions on the Wolverhampton PHV test</a
        >
        come from. Get even one key question wrong and you fail the whole
        test, so make sure these topics are automatic before you book.
      </p>

      <h2>Licensing &amp; Compliance</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>Where should you wear your PHV driver's badge while
        working?</strong>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>In your pocket</li>
        <li>On the upper part of your body, clearly visible</li>
        <li>On the dashboard only</li>
        <li>Hanging from the rear-view mirror</li>
      </ul>
      <details>
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <p>
          <strong>Correct answer: On the upper part of your body, clearly
          visible.</strong> The personal badge must be worn on the upper body
          where it is clearly visible to passengers. Failing to wear it while
          working is an offence.
        </p>
      </details>

      <h2>Customer Care &amp; Safety</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>When your passenger gets out at the end of a journey, what
        should you always do?</strong>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Drive off immediately</li>
        <li>Check the vehicle for any lost property</li>
        <li>Ask the next customer to check</li>
        <li>Take a photo of the back seat</li>
      </ul>
      <details>
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <p>
          <strong>Correct answer: Check the vehicle for any lost
          property.</strong> Drivers must check for lost property at the end
          of every journey and, if found, hand it to a police station within
          24 hours (or follow the council's lost property procedure).
        </p>
      </details>

      <h2>Vehicle &amp; Road Safety</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>What is the maximum number of passengers a saloon car
        licensed as a PHV can carry?</strong>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>3</li>
        <li>4</li>
        <li>5</li>
        <li>8</li>
      </ul>
      <details>
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <p>
          <strong>Correct answer: 4.</strong> A standard saloon PHV is
          licensed for a maximum of 4 passengers. Exceeding this invalidates
          your insurance and is a licensing offence.
        </p>
      </details>

      <h2>Law &amp; Regulations</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>When are you allowed to smoke in a licensed PHV?</strong>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>When you have no passengers</li>
        <li>Never — it is illegal under the Health Act 2006</li>
        <li>Only with the windows open</li>
        <li>Only when off duty but still in the vehicle</li>
      </ul>
      <details>
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <p>
          <strong>Correct answer: Never — it is illegal under the Health Act
          2006.</strong> Smoking (including vaping) in a licensed vehicle is
          illegal at all times under the Health Act 2006 and the smoke-free
          regulations. Penalties include fines and licence action.
        </p>
      </details>

